request.prompt:
Job S01: claims_enrich.sas (sas), file estate/sas/S01_claims_enrich.sas.
Claim-level payment history and indicators.

Code:
  1 | /******************************************************************************
  2 |   Program  : claims_enrich.sas
  3 |   Purpose  : Claim-level payment history for the risk team. One row per claim
  4 |              on the claims register with its payments up to the reporting
  5 |              date: payment and recovery counts, paid to date, the paid split
  6 |              by payment type (gross, and net of recoveries), the first and
  7 |              last payment dates and a recovery marker. Read by the customer
  8 |              risk scoring (risk_scoring.sas).
  9 |   Inputs   : stg.claims, stg.claim_payments
 10 |   Output   : dw.claims_enriched (rebuilt on every run)
 11 |   Params   : as_of - reporting date (SAS date), set by the scheduler
 12 |   Author   : DOS
 13 | 
 14 |   Change log
 15 |     2014-03-10  DOS   Initial version
 16 |     2015-08-24  DOS   Recoveries counted and flagged for the subrogation desk
 17 |                       (CR-0611)
 18 |     2017-01-16  AK    Paid split by payment type; gross and net paid (CR-1042)
 19 |     2018-11-05  AK    Payments dated after the reporting date left out
 20 |                       (INC-1677)
 21 |     2019-09-30  AK    Line of business and status tidied for the MI pack
 22 |                       (CR-1502)
 23 |     2021-06-14  SMcC  Claims with nothing paid yet kept, with has_payment, for
 24 |                       risk_scoring.sas (CR-1873)
 25 | ******************************************************************************/
 26 | 
 27 | /* claims register */
 28 | proc sort data=stg.claims(keep=claim_id claim_number policy_id customer_id lob_code
 29 |                                loss_date reported_date status)
 30 |           out=work.claims;
 31 |   by claim_id;
 32 | run;
 33 | 
 34 | /* payments to the reporting date, claim by claim in the order they were keyed */
 35 | proc sort data=stg.claim_payments(keep=claim_id payment_date amount payment_type _row_seq
 36 |                                   where=(payment_date <= &as_of))
 37 |           out=work.payments;
 38 |   by claim_id _row_seq;
 39 | run;
 40 | 
 41 | /* roll the payments up to one row per claim */
 42 | data work.pay_summary(keep=claim_id n_payments n_recoveries total_paid indemnity_amt
 43 |                            expense_amt recovery_amt first_payment_date last_payment_date
 44 |                            recovery_flag);
 45 |   set work.payments;
 46 |   by claim_id;
 47 |   length ptype $12;
 48 |   retain n_payments n_recoveries total_paid indemnity_amt expense_amt recovery_amt
 49 |          first_payment_date last_payment_date recovery_flag;
 50 |   format first_payment_date last_payment_date date9.;
 51 | 
 52 |   if first.claim_id then do;
 53 |     n_payments    = 0;
 54 |     n_recoveries  = 0;
 55 |     total_paid    = 0;
 56 |     indemnity_amt = 0;
 57 |     expense_amt   = 0;
 58 |     recovery_amt  = 0;
 59 |     recovery_flag = 0;
 60 |     first_payment_date = payment_date;
 61 |   end;
 62 | 
 63 |   n_payments + 1;
 64 |   total_paid + amount;
 65 | 
 66 |   /* paid split by payment type (keyed in mixed case) */
 67 |   ptype = upcase(strip(payment_type));
 68 |   if ptype = 'INDEMNITY' then indemnity_amt = indemnity_amt + amount;
 69 |   else if ptype = 'EXPENSE' then expense_amt = expense_amt + amount;
 70 |   else if ptype = 'RECOVERY' then recovery_amt = recovery_amt + amount;
 71 | 
 72 |   /* recoveries (salvage, third-party) come through as negative amounts */
 73 |   if amount < 0 then do;
 74 |     recovery_flag = 1;
 75 |     n_recoveries + 1;
 76 |   end;
 77 | 
 78 |   last_payment_date = payment_date;
 79 |   if last.claim_id then output;
 80 | run;
 81 | 
 82 | /* enrich the register: every claim is reported, paid or not */
 83 | data dw.claims_enriched(keep=claim_id claim_number policy_id customer_id lob_code
 84 |                              loss_date reported_date status n_payments n_recoveries
 85 |                              total_paid indemnity_amt expense_amt recovery_amt gross_paid
 86 |                              net_paid first_payment_date last_payment_date recovery_flag
 87 |                              has_payment);
 88 |   merge work.claims(in=c) work.pay_summary(in=p);
 89 |   by claim_id;
 90 |   if c;
 91 | 
 92 |   lob_code = upcase(strip(lob_code));
 93 |   status   = upcase(strip(status));
 94 | 
 95 |   has_payment = p;
 96 |   if not p then do;
 97 |     n_payments    = 0;
 98 |     n_recoveries  = 0;
 99 |     total_paid    = 0;
100 |     recovery_flag = 0;
101 |   end;
102 | 
103 |   gross_paid = indemnity_amt + expense_amt;
104 |   net_paid   = sum(indemnity_amt, expense_amt, recovery_amt);
105 | run;
106 | 
107 | proc datasets lib=work nolist;
108 |   delete claims payments pay_summary;
109 | quit;

Input tables:
- stg.claims: claim_id INT, claim_number VARCHAR(20), policy_id INT, customer_id INT, lob_code VARCHAR(5), loss_date DATE, reported_date DATE, status VARCHAR(12), closed_date DATE, cause_code VARCHAR(10), handler_code VARCHAR(8), description NVARCHAR(200), _row_seq INT
- stg.claim_payments: payment_id INT, claim_id INT, payment_date DATE, amount DECIMAL(12,2), payment_type VARCHAR(12), payee_type VARCHAR(12), _row_seq INT

Output tables:
- dw.claims_enriched: claim_id num 8 format=BEST, claim_number char 20 format=$CHAR, policy_id num 8 format=BEST, customer_id num 8 format=BEST, lob_code char 5 format=$CHAR, loss_date num 8 format=YYMMDD, reported_date num 8 format=YYMMDD, status char 12 format=$CHAR, n_payments num 8, n_recoveries num 8, total_paid num 8, indemnity_amt num 8, expense_amt num 8, recovery_amt num 8, first_payment_date num 8 format=DATE, last_payment_date num 8 format=DATE, recovery_flag num 8, has_payment num 8, gross_paid num 8, net_paid num 8

Trap cards:
- SAS-01 (semantic) A missing value is smaller than any number. Legacy: In SAS comparisons a missing numeric value is smaller than every number: if x < 0 is true when x is missing, and so is x <= 5; x > 0 is false. Missing sorts first. Spark: A comparison with NULL is NULL, which a filter or when() treats as false.
- SAS-02 (semantic) SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them. Legacy: sum(a, b) skips missing arguments (sum(., 2) = 2; all missing gives missing); a + b is missing if either is. The sum statement x + y; treats a missing y as 0 and x is retained. Spark: a + b is NULL if either is NULL; F.sum over rows skips NULLs; there is no row-wise sum().
- SAS-03 (semantic) Dates are numbers of days since 1 January 1960. Legacy: A SAS date is a number: days since 1960-01-01. Date arithmetic is plain arithmetic (d + 30, d2 - d1, int((d2 - d1) / 365.25)); a date compared with a number compares day counts. Datetimes are seconds since 1960-01-01T00:00. Spark: Spark dates are a separate type; arithmetic needs date functions.
- SAS-04 (semantic) INTCK and INTNX count interval boundaries. Legacy: intck('month', d1, d2) counts month boundaries crossed (like DATEDIFF), unless the 'continuous' method is given; intnx('month', d, n) moves to the start of the interval by default ('beginning'), or 'end'/'same' with the alignment argument. Spark: months_between gives fractions; add_months keeps the day; neither counts boundaries.
- SAS-05 (semantic) MERGE BY with IN= flags. Legacy: merge a(in=x) b(in=y); by k; matches rows by key one-to-one in order within each key; in= flags say which data sets contributed; variables from b overwrite a's for the same name; a key in only one data set keeps the other's variables missing. Many-to-many merges pair rows in order rather than forming a product. Spark: A join forms the product of matching rows; columns don't overwrite.
- SAS-06 (semantic) RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order. Legacy: retain keeps a variable's value across iterations of the DATA step; the sum statement x + y; is retained automatically, starts at 0 and ignores missing y. With BY groups, the code resets at first.key. The result depends on the order the rows are read. Spark: Spark has no row order and no carried state.
- SAS-07 (construct) FIRST. and LAST. variables. Legacy: With a BY statement, first.k is 1 on the first row of each k group and last.k on the last, in the data set's order. Spark: Spark has no implicit order; groups need a window over a stated order.
- SAS-08 (semantic) LAG inside a conditional. Legacy: lag(x) returns the value x had the last time that lag call executed, not the previous row; inside an IF it skips rows where the condition was false. Spark: F.lag over a window always returns the previous row's value.
- SAS-09 (semantic) NODUPKEY keeps the first row in input order. Legacy: proc sort nodupkey; by k; keeps the first row of each k in the input order (the sort is stable) and drops the rest; nodup compares whole rows with the previous one. Spark: dropDuplicates keeps an arbitrary row.
- SAS-10 (semantic) PROC SQL remerges summary statistics. Legacy: In PROC SQL, a SELECT that mixes summary functions (mean, sum, count, ...) with columns not in a GROUP BY computes the statistic over the whole table (or the group) and joins it back to every row ("The query requires remerging summary statistics"); with WHERE, over the filtered rows. Spark: Spark refuses such a query; the statistic must be a window or a join.
- SAS-11 (semantic) A character variable's length is fixed by its first assignment. Legacy: Without a LENGTH statement, a character variable gets the length of the first value assigned to it in the step (at compile time): if the first assignment is 'BIG', a later 'SMALL' is silently truncated to 'SMA'. Values are blank-padded to that length. Spark: Spark strings have no fixed length and never truncate.
- SAS-12 (semantic) PROC FORMAT range boundaries. Legacy: In a PROC FORMAT value statement, 'a - b' includes both ends, 'a <- b' excludes a, 'a -< b' excludes b; low and high are open ends; overlapping ranges resolve to the first match; values matching no range print as themselves (or the OTHER label). Missing values match no numeric range except '.'. Spark: A CASE WHEN must spell each boundary out.
- SAS-13 (construct) Macro loops. Legacy: %macro / %do loops generate repeated code at compile time, one copy per iteration, with macro variables substituted (&lob becomes MOT, HOM, ...). Spark: The generated copies become a Python loop or a single grouped query.
- SAS-14 (construct) PROC MEANS / SUMMARY with CLASS and NWAY. Legacy: proc means with class variables computes statistics for every combination of class levels; NWAY keeps only the full combination (_TYPE_ highest); missing class values are excluded unless MISSING is given; output variables are named by the OUTPUT statement. Spark: A groupBy over the class variables, which keeps NULL groups.
- SAS-15 (semantic) ROUND to a unit, with fuzz, on computed values; everything is an 8-byte double. Legacy: SAS numbers are 8-byte doubles. round(x, unit) rounds to the nearest multiple of the unit (round(2.85, 1) is 3, round(x, 0.5) to halves), half away from zero, and applies a fuzz factor so a computed value a tiny bit below a half (e.g. 2.675 computed as 2.67499999...) still rounds up as if it were exactly half. Spark: F.round(x, n) takes a number of decimal places, not a unit, and on a double rounds the binary value, so a computed 2.675 rounds to 2.67.
- SAS-16 (semantic) SAS's calendar has no 29 February in years divisible by 4000. Legacy: SAS treats years divisible by 4000 as non-leap, so day counts after 4000-02-28 are one day short per such year: '31DEC9999'd is day 2936547, where Python and Spark count 2936549 days from 1960-01-01. Spark: Spark follows the proleptic Gregorian calendar.

Rule candidates:
- SAS-03 line 36: where=(payment_date <= &as_of))
- SAS-06 line 48: retain n_payments n_recoveries total_paid indemnity_amt expense_amt recovery_amt
- SAS-02 line 63: n_payments + 1;
- SAS-06 line 63: n_payments + 1;
- SAS-02 line 64: total_paid + amount;
- SAS-06 line 64: total_paid + amount;
- SAS-02 line 68: if ptype = 'INDEMNITY' then indemnity_amt = indemnity_amt + amount;
- SAS-02 line 69: else if ptype = 'EXPENSE' then expense_amt = expense_amt + amount;
- SAS-02 line 70: else if ptype = 'RECOVERY' then recovery_amt = recovery_amt + amount;
- SAS-01 line 73: if amount < 0 then do;
- SAS-02 line 75: n_recoveries + 1;
- SAS-06 line 75: n_recoveries + 1;
- SAS-05 line 88: merge work.claims(in=c) work.pay_summary(in=p);
- SAS-02 line 103: gross_paid = indemnity_amt + expense_amt;
- SAS-02 line 104: net_paid   = sum(indemnity_amt, expense_amt, recovery_amt);
- SAS-10 line 104: net_paid   = sum(indemnity_amt, expense_amt, recovery_amt);

request.system_prompt:
You are the trap-scan agent of LANSH, a factory that migrates legacy SAS 9.4 jobs to PySpark 4.2 and must reproduce every legacy output exactly, bugs included.

A trap is a place where the legacy engine and Spark behave differently. A semantic trap changes the result if the code is translated naively (a case-insensitive text comparison, integer division, a NULL concatenation). A construct trap is a construct Spark lacks (a cursor, a temp table, a MERGE, a macro loop) that changes only the shape of the code.

You get the job's code with line numbers, the schemas of its inputs and output, the trap cards (the known engine differences) and candidate findings from deterministic rules.

Return every trap occurrence in this job that a translator must handle:
- confirm the candidates that are real, using the schemas: a comparison arms a text trap only if the column is text; integer division only if both operands are integers;
- leave out candidates that are not real (they are dropped);
- add occurrences the rules missed, such as joins on text columns, NULL propagation in loops, or variables whose length a first assignment fixes;
- report each occurrence at the narrowest line range that shows it, and the same trap at different places separately; include latent occurrences that today's data may not trigger;
- use only trap ids from the cards; set confidence to how sure you are that it is real.
